. * HYPOTHESIS TEST FOR H2 * 
.  
.         * We standardize responses to plh0004, a question that asks responden
> ts to classify their political attitude on a scale from 0 (far left) to 10 (f
> ar right). We reverse code and standardized responses to plh0004 to construct
>  our dependent variable, std_left_lean.  We take this to a good measure of le
> ft-leaning political preferences (H2). If H2 is true and having daughters lea
> ds to more left leaning political preferences, respondents will be more likel
> y to classify their political attitudes as closer to 0 when responding to plh
> 0004. We estimate the parameter of interest, the coefficient on NumDaughters,
>  from a linear OLS regression analysis with std_left_lean as dependent variab
> le and the count variable num_daughters as independent variable, controlling 
> for demographic characteristics, missing data, country of origin, state of re
> sidence, total family size, and survey wave. We weight responses using the in
> dividual weight (phrf) provided by the SOEP.
. 
.         reg std_left_lean num_daughters r_* mi_* i.corigin i.syear i.hgnuts1 
> num_kids [aweight=phrf] if num_kids!=0, r
